# CODEOWNERS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real-org extension pattern (not active in this 2-account demo environment):
# once a second real reviewer exists, hand /PFL/notebooks/ (day-to-day
# notebook code) to them directly - it's already split out below so this
# requires no restructuring, just changing the owner on one line.
# Prod-affecting paths (bundle target config, job resources, pipeline
# definitions) stay with a stricter/senior owner even after that split.
#
# Example with real teams in a larger org:
#   /databricks.yml         @org/prod-approvers
#   /PFL/resources/          @org/prod-approvers
#   /PFL/notebooks/           @org/qa-approvers
#   /.github/                 @org/platform-team
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Live demo state: 2 real accounts (stuti404, Stuti-Ganit). stuti404 is the
# sole approver everywhere because Stuti-Ganit is the primary PR author for
# dev/uat and GitHub structurally forbids self-approval - adding
# Stuti-Ganit as a CODEOWNERS entry here would not add a real second
# reviewer, it would silently fall back to stuti404 anyway. Per-branch
# strictness is instead enforced via branch-protection settings
# (enforce_admins, linear history on main) and the production GitHub
# Environment's required-reviewer gate on publish-prod, not by forking this
# file's content across branches.

/databricks.yml       @stuti404
/PFL/resources/       @stuti404
/PFL/notebooks/       @stuti404
/.github/             @stuti404
